00:00:00.007 --> 00:00:02.340
MARK MIRCHANDANI: With almost
500 sessions at Next 2019,

00:00:02.340 --> 00:00:04.590
it's understandable that you
couldn't get to them all.

00:00:04.590 --> 00:00:07.560
So if you didn't see best
practices, GCP resource

00:00:07.560 --> 00:00:09.308
organization, and
access management,

00:00:09.308 --> 00:00:11.100
then stay tuned, because
here's the rewind.

00:00:11.100 --> 00:00:13.012
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:15.533 --> 00:00:17.200
Regardless if you're
new to Google Cloud

00:00:17.200 --> 00:00:19.090
Platform or a
longtime user, it's

00:00:19.090 --> 00:00:22.340
important to keep all your
GCP resources organized.

00:00:22.340 --> 00:00:24.130
We don't just mean
resources like VMs,

00:00:24.130 --> 00:00:26.680
but actually at the project
and organization level.

00:00:26.680 --> 00:00:28.450
In this talk, Max
and Greg go over

00:00:28.450 --> 00:00:31.210
what these different resources
are and best practices

00:00:31.210 --> 00:00:32.650
for organizing them.

00:00:32.650 --> 00:00:35.080
It's important to consider
the setup since almost a third

00:00:35.080 --> 00:00:37.540
of billing support cases
that we handle end up being

00:00:37.540 --> 00:00:39.112
resource management related.

00:00:39.112 --> 00:00:41.320
If you're not sure how your
resources are configured,

00:00:41.320 --> 00:00:44.200
you may find yourself dealing
with confusing invoices, access

00:00:44.200 --> 00:00:47.020
issues, or even potential
disruption of service.

00:00:47.020 --> 00:00:48.610
The best way to
avoid these issues is

00:00:48.610 --> 00:00:50.990
to make sure everything is
set up to match your needs.

00:00:50.990 --> 00:00:52.900
Let's take a look at
the resource hierarchy.

00:00:52.900 --> 00:00:54.910
Here's an overview of
the different resources.

00:00:54.910 --> 00:00:57.285
We'll step into each area and
talk about different things

00:00:57.285 --> 00:00:58.240
to consider.

00:00:58.240 --> 00:01:00.340
Your domain and organization
are like an umbrella

00:01:00.340 --> 00:01:01.960
for everything in the cloud.

00:01:01.960 --> 00:01:04.510
The domain is done through
G Suite or Cloud Identity,

00:01:04.510 --> 00:01:06.010
and helps you manage users.

00:01:06.010 --> 00:01:08.920
Organizations let you manage
all of the GCP resources

00:01:08.920 --> 00:01:10.000
through the console.

00:01:10.000 --> 00:01:12.610
Together, these make sure you
have a way to manage everything

00:01:12.610 --> 00:01:15.070
from a high level, especially
helpful for situations

00:01:15.070 --> 00:01:17.170
when someone may have
lost access to a low level

00:01:17.170 --> 00:01:19.190
resource like a project.

00:01:19.190 --> 00:01:20.890
You'll have a super
admin for the domain

00:01:20.890 --> 00:01:23.410
and an organizational admin
for your organization, which

00:01:23.410 --> 00:01:24.670
could be the same person.

00:01:24.670 --> 00:01:27.280
Make sure to consider who should
be the admin in each case,

00:01:27.280 --> 00:01:28.707
such as the org
admin being a more

00:01:28.707 --> 00:01:30.790
senior technical member
in charge of all the cloud

00:01:30.790 --> 00:01:31.752
projects.

00:01:31.752 --> 00:01:33.460
It's also key to make
sure everyone knows

00:01:33.460 --> 00:01:36.280
who the admins for these are
and to keep all the rolls up

00:01:36.280 --> 00:01:37.150
to date.

00:01:37.150 --> 00:01:39.370
Try to aim for a reasonable
level of redundancy

00:01:39.370 --> 00:01:41.920
to avoid any single
points of failure.

00:01:41.920 --> 00:01:44.260
Billing accounts are under
the organization and control

00:01:44.260 --> 00:01:47.198
how you pay for anything
the associated projects use.

00:01:47.198 --> 00:01:49.240
They have their own set
of roles and permissions,

00:01:49.240 --> 00:01:51.130
such as the billing
account users,

00:01:51.130 --> 00:01:53.530
who can see how much each
project is spending and link

00:01:53.530 --> 00:01:55.240
projects to a billing account.

00:01:55.240 --> 00:01:57.820
Only a billing account
admin can remove projects

00:01:57.820 --> 00:02:00.130
from the billing
account and set budgets.

00:02:00.130 --> 00:02:02.650
Again, it's important to know
who your billing admin is,

00:02:02.650 --> 00:02:04.275
because they're needed
if you ever want

00:02:04.275 --> 00:02:05.620
to contact billing support.

00:02:05.620 --> 00:02:07.870
In most cases, you'll only
need one billing account

00:02:07.870 --> 00:02:09.367
per organization,
which will also

00:02:09.367 --> 00:02:11.200
help keep things simple
when looking at what

00:02:11.200 --> 00:02:12.760
you're paying per project.

00:02:12.760 --> 00:02:15.670
Don't forget to enable BigQuery
Export as soon as possible,

00:02:15.670 --> 00:02:18.910
so you can further analyze
what you're paying for.

00:02:18.910 --> 00:02:21.905
Now, the payments profile
actually sits outside the cloud

00:02:21.905 --> 00:02:24.280
and is used for paying anything
in Google, such as Chrome

00:02:24.280 --> 00:02:24.953
licenses.

00:02:24.953 --> 00:02:27.370
You may want to have the same
admin for both your payments

00:02:27.370 --> 00:02:29.260
profile and your
billing account.

00:02:29.260 --> 00:02:32.170
But you can customize who has
access to edit information

00:02:32.170 --> 00:02:34.392
or only have read only access.

00:02:34.392 --> 00:02:36.850
Just like your billing account,
we typically recommend only

00:02:36.850 --> 00:02:38.620
having one payment profile.

00:02:38.620 --> 00:02:40.300
Making sure the right
people have access

00:02:40.300 --> 00:02:42.550
and getting notifications
can go a long way

00:02:42.550 --> 00:02:44.200
in avoiding any fire drills.

00:02:44.200 --> 00:02:46.180
You should also add a
secondary payment method

00:02:46.180 --> 00:02:48.013
and review the roles
here on a regular basis

00:02:48.013 --> 00:02:50.560
to avoid any potential problems.

00:02:50.560 --> 00:02:52.120
Anything done in
GCP will be done

00:02:52.120 --> 00:02:53.710
from a project,
which are basically

00:02:53.710 --> 00:02:55.720
like groupings of
direct cloud resources,

00:02:55.720 --> 00:02:57.520
such as Kubernetes clusters.

00:02:57.520 --> 00:03:00.430
These projects can exist under
folders as well as be labeled,

00:03:00.430 --> 00:03:03.040
so you have full customization
over your hierarchy.

00:03:03.040 --> 00:03:04.780
In fact, we recommend
trying to match

00:03:04.780 --> 00:03:06.110
your company's environment.

00:03:06.110 --> 00:03:08.170
So maybe you'd have a
folder for each team

00:03:08.170 --> 00:03:10.545
or different folders
for dev and prod.

00:03:10.545 --> 00:03:11.920
You should use
naming conventions

00:03:11.920 --> 00:03:14.290
that are familiar to your
team for projects and labels,

00:03:14.290 --> 00:03:16.360
so everyone knows what
each project is for.

00:03:16.360 --> 00:03:18.520
Labels can help
group those together.

00:03:18.520 --> 00:03:20.590
Each project also has
its own role management,

00:03:20.590 --> 00:03:22.810
so you can ensure only
trusted people can

00:03:22.810 --> 00:03:24.520
edit resources
inside of a project

00:03:24.520 --> 00:03:26.785
or even create new projects.

00:03:26.785 --> 00:03:28.910
This is a quick overview
of the resource hierarchy,

00:03:28.910 --> 00:03:31.090
but there's plenty more
specifics and best practices

00:03:31.090 --> 00:03:33.298
to check out, which you can
read through in the guide

00:03:33.298 --> 00:03:34.810
and checklist linked here.

00:03:34.810 --> 00:03:37.090
Max and Greg go into a
deeper dive for each area

00:03:37.090 --> 00:03:39.322
during the talk, cover
more best practices,

00:03:39.322 --> 00:03:40.780
and walk through
an example of what

00:03:40.780 --> 00:03:43.852
a company might consider for
their resources as they grow.

00:03:43.852 --> 00:03:45.310
If you'd like to
see the full talk,

00:03:45.310 --> 00:03:47.300
check out the link in
the description below.

00:03:47.300 --> 00:03:49.717
And don't forget to subscribe
